<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Said bin Sultan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sayyid Said bin Sultan, Said ibn Sultan, Sayyid Said of Oman · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-said-dynasty">Al Bu Said dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Said bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> from 1806 to 1856, the most powerful ruler of the Omani maritime empire. He extended Omani control along the East African coast, taking Mombasa from the Mazrui in 1837, and moved his court to Zanzibar, where he developed clove plantations and the caravan trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves. After his death his realm was divided, creating the separate Sultanate of Zanzibar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/said-bin-sultan.jpg" alt="Said bin Sultan" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lieutenant Henry Blosse Lynch · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Said_bin_Sultan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sayyid Said bin Sultan</b>, ruler of Oman from 1806, turned that claim into an empire: he subdued the rival Mazrui governors of Mombasa by 1837, encouraged the planting of cloves on Zanzibar and Pemba, and from about 1840 made Zanzibar town his principal residence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sayyid Said of Oman</b> conquered Mombasa in 1837 and deported leading Mazrui.</blockquote><a href="/state/mazrui-sultanate">Mazrui Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Said ibn Sultan</b>, ruling from 1806 to 1856, built up Zanzibar, planting cloves and promoting the caravan trade in ivory and slaves, and moved his residence there around 1840.</blockquote><a href="/state/omani-empire">Omani Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Said ibn Sultan</b> of the Omani Empire died in 1856, his son Thuwaini took Muscat and another son Majid took Zanzibar.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.61,58.59&amp;year=1833" aria-label="Muscat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6112 1293 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Said bin Sultan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6562 1563h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6562 1563h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.61,58.59&amp;year=1833">What was here in 1833? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
